- F-351 **A compiled transaction validates itself and every ancestor, and an abort restarts the block whose transaction it names.** A compiled `atomic` block's transaction records no read of its own write; it records a read of an ancestor's write with that write, a read of the committed value with that value, and a read of an empty array element with a mark. Validation, at a read once the global lock has moved and at a commit that did not get the lock at its snapshot, covers the transaction and every ancestor. An abort names the transaction whose read failed; `abort()` names none. The block's handler takes every exception (`BaseTask.leaveTransaction`): it ends the block's transaction, starts the block again for an abort that names that transaction or names none (or any abort at the outermost block), commits the transaction on an exit, a `LabelExit`, and throws the exit on, or takes the abort path where that commit fails (F-401), and throws everything else on, after a nested transaction has handed its reads to its parent. A nested commit hands its parent its reads and its writes, also where it wrote nothing. The exception-table entry of a block or a `try` follows its body's code, so a nested one is taken first. Open: a nested block sees a plain write by another thread that runs its outer block (row 984). Source: `ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/runtimeSystem/Transaction.java:228-367`, `runtimeSystem/BaseTask.java:295-322`,`compiler/codegen/CodeGen.java:2148-2180` (`forAtomicBlock`), `:2490-2494` (`forTry`). Tests: `compiler_tests/AtomicOwnWriteNestedCompiled`, `AtomicNestedSameBodyCompiled`, `NestedAtomicAbortCompiled`, `AtomicNestedViewCompiled`, `AtomicSharedReadsCompiled`, `AtomicThrowRevertsCompiled`, `AtomicNestedReadsKeptCompiled`, `NestedTryInnerCatch`; `ArrayStoreJUTest`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-compiled-transaction/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-exit-out-of-atomic/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 0a83a7049; note: the clause that climb batch 35's merged-diff review added on a failed commit at an exit given its verb ("a commit that fails taking the abort path" had none); meaning unchanged

- F-397 **A numeral with a radix point keeps its digits as written in the node that both paths read, so its digit count `n` counts its leading zeros: `00.5` is `RadixPointNumeral[\3, 1, 10, 5\]`.** It builds P-174's digit count. `ExprFactory.makeFloatLiteralExpr` removes only apostrophes and U+202F from the numeral's text (`nodes_util/ExprFactory.java:1637-1638`), as `makeIntLiteralExpr` does, and `NumeralNumbers.of` counts `n` from that text for the checker and for walk (`nodes_util/NumeralNumbers.java:71-100`); the value, `m` and `v` are read as before. A message that quotes the numeral shows it as written. A folded `MIN`, `MAX` or `|x|` whose answer is a written radix-point operand keeps that operand's text, so its numeral type in a checker message counts the operand's zeros; a folded `+` takes its value's digits. This rule for folded constants is a worker's choice, not a decision of the curator; no coercion reads a folded constant's numbers (P-174). Tests: `tests/RadixPointLeadingZerosWalk.fss`, `compiler_tests/RadixPointLeadingZerosCompiled.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-radix-point-leading-zeros/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why" and "Every reader of the node's text, and what it now sees".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-radix-point-leading-zeros/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; from: coordinator/PLAN.md, "Climb batch 35, listed for his review"; commit: 0a83a7049; note: the record's form of the gather's draft of climb batch 35: the decision it builds named; the folded operand's text stated as R.worker.1's default, not consequential, recorded here by the review queue's rule; test and report paths in the record's short form

- F-398 **Walk applies a subscript that is a math item with all its indices, and binds a tuple binding's names from one run of its right side.** `x[5]^2` is `(x[5])^2`, `m[3,7]^2` is `(m[3,7])^2`, `g[3][7]` is `(g[3])[7]` and `x[5]k` is `(x[5]) k` under walk as on the compiled path (F-387): `evalAndInvokeMethod`, the one helper of `mathItemApplication`, evaluates every index as `forSubscriptExpr` does (`interpreter/evaluator/Evaluator.java:1069-1071`). A tuple binding at the top level or as a field of an object or object expression runs its right side once: a tuple expression of as many elements as names binds each name to its own element, and any other right side binds a temporary declared at the names' types, which each name reads through the desugarer's own `Local` or `Member` reference (`interpreter/rewrite/DesugarerVisitor.java:854`, `:868-877`, `:878-893`, `:897`), as the compiled path does (F-369); a numeral converts to its name's declared type, as at a single binding. Where such a right side does not convert to the names' types, walk's message names the temporary, not the name, as 'Type mismatch binding (5,6) ... to *tuple_<span> (type (NN32,String))'; this message is a worker's choice, not a decision of the curator. An array literal inside a right side that is not a tuple expression, bound at an array type, is not built and stops at load (row 1105). So a program for both paths writes these forms unparenthesised, and binds a call's tuple to names directly. Tests: `tests/SubscriptThenSuperscriptWalk.fss`, `tests/TopLevelTupleBindingOnceWalk.fss`, `tests/FieldTupleBindingOnceWalk.fss`, `tests/TupleBindingNumeralTypesWalk.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-subscript-tuple-binding/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why" and "Old against new".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-walk-subscript-tuple-binding/JUDGE.md, "What lands unrepaired"; from: coordinator/PLAN.md, "Climb batch 35, listed for his review"; commit: 0a83a7049; note: the message that names the temporary, W.reserved.4 and W.judge.2 of climb batch 35's rung W, a default that is not consequential, recorded here by the review queue's rule (`PLAN.md:938`; `JUDGE.md:57`; `SKEPTIC.md:79`); no other entry or row held it

- F-399 **The dispatch method of a trait's or an object's functional method reads each argument at its own local, wherever self stands.** The method is an instance method of the receiver's class, with self in local 0 and the other arguments after it in their order. `OverloadSet.argumentLocal`, overridden in `ForTraitOrObject`, gives an argument before self's place the local after its own place, for the type tests and the casts alike (`compiler/OverloadSet.java:2357-2363`, `:2495-2508`, `:1538`, `:1876`). A call reaches the dispatch method where the declaration that the top-level dispatch of the name chooses has a more specific one below it in the type, as an inherited one does; a declaration of the type with none below it is called at its exact signature, its body. It does not reach two forms: a generic functional method overloaded beside a plain one, on which the code generator stops first (row 724), and a generic functional method with self not first, which dies at another site (row 594). So an object or trait may overload an inherited functional method with self anywhere. Tests: `compiler_tests/FunctionalMethodOverloadSelfLast`, `FunctionalMethodCoverSelfSecond`, `FunctionalMethodSelfSecondReceiverTested`, `FunctionalMethodMeetNarrowedSelfNotFirst`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-self-not-first-dispatch/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-self-not-first-dispatch/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 0a83a7049; note: the record's form of the gather's draft of climb batch 35: one claim to a sentence, "the method" and "Not reached" given their nouns; meaning unchanged

- F-400 **The code generator compiles the modifier `override` by the checker's table of what an override keeps from being inherited, and the checker refuses an `override` that overrides nothing.** A trait's or an object's class holds the override and, at each hidden declaration's JVM signature, a bridge that runs it; no method runs the hidden declaration, and a dispatch over the override and the declarations of its name that it does not override is still written (`scala_src/useful/STypesUtil.scala:1798-1812`, `overriddenNotProvided`; `compiler/codegen/CodeGen.java:1224-1237`, `overrideBridges`; `:1476-1484`, `dumpOverloadedMethodChaining`). The checker refuses an `override` that appears in no overriding pair of `providedAndOverridden`, equal parameter types included, as the traits chapter and walk do: 'Invalid override of f in object B: ... has the modifier override and does not override any inherited declaration.' (`scala_src/typechecker/OverloadingChecker.scala:424`, `:763-789`). Two forms stay code-generator walls: an override of or by a method with static parameters of its own, and one in a generic type whose parameter types name a static parameter (row 1106). The checker refuses an override that overrides a declaration inherited under a conditional extension only by the clause's bound, which walk runs (row 1107), and does not check an override in an object expression (row 1108). Under walk an object's override hides every inherited declaration of its name (row 1109). So a program may write `override` on the compiled path wherever no static parameter is involved. Tests: `compiler_tests/OverrideModifierCodeGeneration`, `OverrideBridgesCompiled`, `XXXOverrideNothingCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-override-compiled/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-override-compiled/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; from: compile-ladder/climb-batch-35/JUDGE-review.md, "The repair"; commit: 0a83a7049; note: the record's form of the gather's draft of climb batch 35, as the repair on the merged tree narrowed it: its sentence of four rows split in three, each clause kept; meaning unchanged

- F-401 **An exit out of an `atomic` block commits the block's transaction and keeps its writes, on both paths.** Compiled, the block's handler `BaseTask.leaveTransaction` commits on a `LabelExit` as `endTransaction` does at a normal end (`runtimeSystem/BaseTask.java:303-309`); walk's `FortressTaskRunner.doItOnce` commits on a `LabelException` as at a normal end (`interpreter/evaluator/tasks/FortressTaskRunner.java:133-141`). A commit that fails runs the block again, as at a normal end, and an exception other than an exit still discards the writes. A nested block's commit hands its writes to the outer one, whose commit on the same exit stores them. Neither path tells an exit to an enclosing label from one to a label block that has completed, so such a late exit also commits the atomic blocks it passes (row 1041). Under walk an exit or an exception out of a `tryatomic` block that no `atomic` block encloses is wrapped in a `RuntimeException` and reaches neither its label nor a `catch` (rows 1110 and 1111). So a program may exit out of an atomic block (`Specification/basic/expressions/atomic.tex:59-63`). Tests: `tests/ExitOutOfAtomicWalk.fss`, `compiler_tests/ExitOutOfAtomicCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-exit-out-of-atomic/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why" and "The tests: failing on the base, passing after".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-exit-out-of-atomic/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 0a83a7049; note: the record's form of the gather's draft of climb batch 35: "the latter" given its noun; the sentence on row 1041 states the tree, and A.worker.1's question on it is review-queue item 562's; meaning unchanged

- P-174 **A numeral's type exists only at compile time: the Working Draft's `NaturalNumeral` or, with a radix point, `RadixPointNumeral`, below `Numeral`, with its four numbers as static parameters; every number type, the library's too, takes a numeral through a coercion generic over the four numbers.** The four numbers are the digit count `n`, the digits after the radix point `m`, the radix `r` and the digits read as one integer in that radix `v` (`Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/expressions/literals.tex:83-129`), so `3.125` is a `RadixPointNumeral[\4, 3, 10, 3125\]`; the four are naturals of any size; this implementation's `NN32` bound (P-40) is for sizes. A coercion's body is a computation at compile time, with no method invocation: a number used as a value there converts where it stands, as a numeral does (P-173), into the type that its place fixes, and is refused statically where it does not fit, so `value object Byte(b: ZZ32)` declares `coerce[\nat n, nat r, nat v\](x: NaturalNumeral[\n, r, v\]) where { v < 256 } = Byte(v)`; where nothing fixes a type, it is an exact integer, `ZZ`. The coercion's body calls the type's constructor on values computed from the four numbers, as in `coerce[\nat n, nat m, nat r, nat v\](x: RadixPointNumeral[\n, m, r, v\]) = Interval(roundDown(v, r, m), roundUp(v, r, m))`, and the compiler instantiates it for the numeral and folds it (P-176). No numeral value exists when a program runs: a numeral type has no getters, no `asFloat`, no `asString` and no `=` with a number type, and is written only as a coercion's or an operator's parameter type. A numeral has no sign: at a program's type `-3` is the type's own prefix `-` applied to the coercion's result for `3`, and a type without `-` refuses it; any other expression of numerals alone folds first (P-173) and reaches such a type only through its coercion from the folded value's type, such as `QQ` for `1/2`, since coercions do not chain. The design leaves out the binary chapter's numeral coercion and `opr ||` (`Specification-1.0-frozen/advanced-lib/binary.tex:1620-1662`). Why: the Draft gives numerals types of their own so that "library designers [can] decide how numerals should interact with other types of objects by defining coercion operations", and has libraries define the coercions into integers and rationals (`literals.tex:132-148`); the curator finishes that design, in place of the implementations' `IntLiteral` (P-76) and `FloatLiteral` (P-172). Row 981 holds where the tree departs: walk running a numeral type written in a `comprises` clause. The tree departs too, with no row, where the numeral coercions of the one library and of many tests call a getter on the four numbers, as `v.asZZ32`, and where the checker and the literals chapter read each of the four as a `ZZ` value wherever it stands (F-342, F-345).
  from: reviews/batch-35-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; from: coordinator/HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-11-23)"; commit: 0a83a7049; note: the last sentence brought in line with the tree by the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 ("you don't need my approval to bring the record in line with reality"): since the rewrite of `be43ddc647` the tree departs at more places than row 981: `Library/FortressLibrary.fss:388-389`, `:847`, `:954`, `:1065`; `KindEnv.scala`, `getType` (F-342); `Specification/basic/expressions/literals.tex:186-188`; 13 test files, rung R's two among them. The decision, its reach and its requirement are unchanged; the coordinator names the change to him with its old and new words

- P-40 **Sizes are checked, and a size that inference leaves unknown is an error only where it is used.** A size is a `nat` or `int` static parameter, such as the length of an array in its type. The compiled checker checks sizes, and refuses a size beyond `NN32` or `ZZ32` (F-140, the bound on both paths). The bound is this implementation's, for the sizes of arrays, vectors and matrices, to match the JVM's `int` indices; a numeral type's four static parameters have none, and read as values convert where they stand, as a numeral does, a `ZZ` where nothing fixes a type (P-174). Wherever the array design, the compiled path's design for arrays (P-85), creates a JVM array, it checks the length there, at run time, and refuses one over 2^31-1. A size that inference leaves unknown is an error only when it reaches a type, a name or a value. So a declaration may declare a size that none of its types uses, and pass. A call that cannot fix a size of the declaration that it would choose is refused, also inside an overload set, instead of passing over that declaration. In the rule that no type extends two different instantiations of one generic trait, two instantiations differ when any static argument but an operator differs, so `Vec[\3\]` and `Vec[\4\]` are two; boolean arguments count too once the checker has boolean parameters. A size used as a number, as in the range `0#n`, converts to `ZZ32` where one is needed, as a numeral does. The 17 vector and matrix operators of the `FortressLibrary` api declare only the sizes that their types use, the change of `explorations/perf-probes/array-sites/e3-rebased.patch`. It finishes the team's own fix of 2008, which removed those sizes from the component only. The JVM length check waits for the array design; under walk such an array stops at its store's factory with walk's own error, which no catch sees (row 527). The checker still passes over a declaration whose unfixed size occurs in its own parameter type (row 400). The checker compares no boolean arguments (row 406).
  from: coordinator/HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-11-23)"; commit: 0a83a7049; note: the clause that restated P-174 as it stood before `be43ddc647` brought in line with it, by his "YES!!" of 13:0x UTC on 2026-11-23 to that reading (P-174's history); P-40's own decision, the sizes and their bound, unchanged; the coordinator names the change to him

- P-173 **A numeral converts where it stands: where its place fixes a type, into that type at compile time from its exact value; where nothing fixes one, into the machine default, a `ZZ32`, or a `ZZ64` or `ZZ` when its value does not fit, and with a radix point an `RR64`.** A place fixes a type when its declared type has a coercion from the numeral type, as the eight number types have and a program's own type may (P-174); `Any`, `Object`, `Number`, an unbounded type parameter and no declared type take the default. So `x = 3` is a `ZZ32`, and `x SEQV z` is `true` for `z: ZZ32 = 3`; `bigN = 3000000000` is a `ZZ64` at its binding (row 946). The default applies to a numeral, a literal after the fold, never to an expression of a numeral type, and a `nat` parameter used as a value is a `ZZ32` where it is a size (P-40), and, where it is one of a numeral type's four numbers, converts where it stands, a `ZZ` where nothing fixes a type (P-174). A type parameter that several numerals fix takes the narrowest default that holds every value, an `RR64` where any has a radix point, each numeral converting into it exactly; a float numeral whose conversions into `QQ` and `RR64` tie reads `RR64`. At a typed place the conversion is exact or rounds once (P-76, P-172), and a numeral outside the type's range is refused statically; walk converts from the exact value at the same places, place by place, before it runs the checker (P-12). An expression of numerals alone folds exactly before the checker into a signed constant, which defaults as an integer numeral, as a `QQ` for a quotient, or as an `RR64` with a radix point: so `x = 7/2` is a `QQ`, `1/2 = 0.5` is `true`, and `-2147483648`, prefix minus on a numeral, folded, is a `ZZ32`. `gg(5, w)`, with `gg` declared at `(ZZ32, T)` and at `(Any, Any)`, runs the `ZZ32` declaration (P-49). The parameter types of the declaration that a call runs are places that fix a type, and a numeral argument goes to the most specific declaration that takes it, by its own conversion if need be, with its default as the last resort: `u = 0` is `true` for `u: NN32 = 0`, the numeral an `NN32` at `NN32`'s own `=` (P-46 gives the rule and its ground; row 1016 holds where walk departs, and row 1017 where both paths do). Why: the design completes the Working Draft's, whose numerals have types of their own so that a library decides by coercions how they meet other types (P-174 gives that ground). It bends P-26 in two places, the numeral types, whose names and static parameters are the Draft's (P-174), and the places that fix no type, where the compiler's prelude, the team's later word, keeps the numeral's own type; the curator takes the bend by his right to finish the specification. He declined exact defaults, `ZZ` and `QQ` (the 2012 restart gives `NN` and `QQ`, `literals.tick:25`), since microGPT's untyped hyperparameters (`MicroGptFlat.fss:18`) would then be rationals and every untyped index would need `asZZ32`. The Draft's text that it contradicts, for Appendix I: numerals "not directly converted" and "polymorphic" (`Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/expressions/literals.tex:132-141`) at places that fix no type, and a `nat` parameter usable wherever an `NN32` variable is (`basic/trait-parameters.tex:84-86`); P-174 names the binary chapter's, which it leaves out. What it keeps and changes in form: the judgement's section 3, but for the numeral types' form, which P-174 holds. Detail: `reviews/literal-design-judgement-2026-11-08.md`, sections 1 to 6 and the 42 test programs of its appendix; the prototype, `compile-ladder/plan-29/probes/literal-default.md`.
  from: coordinator/HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-11-23)"; commit: 0a83a7049; note: the half-clause that restated P-174 as it stood before `be43ddc647` brought in line with it, by his "YES!!" of 13:0x UTC on 2026-11-23 to that reading (P-174's history); the size half and the rest of P-173 unchanged; the coordinator names the change to him

- F-342 **The compiled checker converts a numeral where nothing fixes a type into its default, refuses one out of range, and refuses a numeral type written other than as a parameter's type.** Where no place's type has a coercion from the numeral's type (an untyped binding, `Any`, `Object`, `Number`, an unbounded type parameter, a receiver, a join, a result with no declared return type), a numeral becomes a `ZZ32`, or a `ZZ64` or `ZZ` by its value, or an `RR64` with a radix point. A type parameter that numerals alone fix takes the narrowest default holding them all, and the conversion into the default ranks as no coercion. A numeral outside a bounded number type's range, or rounding to an infinity, is a static error, also as a tuple's element at a tuple type and in a result position of an `if`, a `case`, a `do` block or a `try`; a program's type with no coercion from the numeral's type refuses a numeral. `NaturalNumeral` and `RadixPointNumeral` are written in a program only as a parameter's type, never as a type that an object or a trait extends. A `nat` or `int` parameter read as a value is a `ZZ32`, but a `ZZ` where it is one of a numeral coercion's four numbers (`scala_src/typechecker/staticenv/KindEnv.scala`, `getType`). It builds P-173's checker, P-174's written-only rule and P-40's size value, but not P-174's four numbers, which P-174 converts where they stand (no row); a `typecase`'s selector keeps its own numeral type (row 963). Tests: `compiler_tests/LiteralDefaultCompiled`, `LiteralDefaultResultCompiled`, `XXXLiteralTypeUnwritable.test`, `XXXNumeralBeyondZZ32Range.test`, `XXXNumeralBeyondRangeInTuple.test`, `XXXNumeralJoinBeyondRange.test`, `XXXNumeralTypeExtendedRefused.test`, `NatValueZZ32Compiled`, `NumeralStaticDesignCompiled` (a `v` beyond `ZZ64`). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-checker/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-35-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; from: coordinator/HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-11-23)"; commit: 0a83a7049; note: "It builds ... P-174's ... four numbers" said more than the tree once `be43ddc647` made P-174 convert each of the four where it stands; the checker's reading, the sentence before, is unchanged

- F-345 **The specification states, in the revision form, the static numerals (P-174, P-176) and P-171's string literals.** The literals chapter gives a numeral a numeral type that exists only at compile time, `NaturalNumeral[\n, r, v\]` or `RadixPointNumeral[\n, m, r, v\]` below a `Numeral` without static parameters, the four numbers its static arguments, naturals of any size, and every number type's coercion generic over the four numbers, read as `ZZ` and folded, one value per place; P-174 instead converts each of the four where it stands (no row). It also gives the conversion where a place fixes a type and the default elsewhere; a program's type receiving a numeral as written, its minus on the coercion's result, any other static expression through its coercion from the default's type; a `where` clause over the four numbers refusing statically; and the numeral types written only as parameter types (`Specification/basic/expressions/literals.tex:98-285`). The constant chapter gives the fold, converted from its exact value at a library type (`constant.tex:80-126`). The numbers chapter gives the coercions, with no `=` pairs and no `asFloat` on a numeral type (`basic-lib/numbers.tex:47-71`). The binary chapter's numeral declarations are expressible now and left out. Appendix I gives `revival-numerals` and `revival-string-literals` (`Specification/appendices/changes.tex`), whose Effects name each departure of the two implementations with its row. Test: the specification's build (`ant genSource`, `ant tex`), 0 warnings by the skill's grep. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-static-numerals-spec/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-35-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; from: coordinator/HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-11-23)"; commit: 0a83a7049; note: the text's reading of the four numbers as `ZZ` values (`literals.tex:186-188`) departs from P-174 since its rewrite at `be43ddc647`; one clause says so

- F-323 **The floats' natives over `Number` and `AnyIntegral` are declared at `RR64` or convert in Fortress, and group 3's six name one-line helpers.** `random`, `RR64`'s `atan2`, `printThreadInfo` and `RR64`'s `^` take an `RR64`, which coercion reaches from a `ZZ32`, an `RR32` or a numeral; another number type writes `asFloat(x)`, where P-165 names the getter `x.asRR64`, which no type declares (no row). `RR64`'s power over `AnyIntegral` converts its exponent by each type's own `asFloat` around a public bare `^` at `ZZ32` on `simpleDoubleArith.doublePowInt` (P-162). `RR64`'s and `RR32`'s mixed powers, `RR64`'s floor and ceiling brackets, and `FlatString`'s `=` and `flatConcat` at a `Char` name new helpers that compute as their glue did, `flatConcat` past U+FFFF keeping the low 16 bits (row 872). `printThreadInfo` at `RR64` is Fortress through `asString`, so a numeral prints as a float, `3.0`. F-299 and F-32 give the library's counts of bindings. Test: `tests/NativeHelperFloatMixedWalk`, `tests/NativeHelperRationalArgumentWalk`, `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilyFloatMixedCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-natives-float-number/REPORT.md`, sections 1, 5 and 6.
  from: coordinator/HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-11-23)"; commit: 0a83a7049; note: "(P-165)" pointed to a decision that, since `be43ddc647`, names the getter `asRR64`; the one library still declares `asFloat` (`BOOT.md:14`: "`asFloat` renamed on the eight types, about 30 lines of the one library, 51 test and demo files")

- F-324 **The one library's `Number` declares nothing; each number type declares its own `asFloat` and an `=` at each number type it coerces from or into.** It builds P-160's second step and P-165, but for P-165's name: the types declare `asFloat`, not the getter `asRR64` (no row). `Number`'s `comprises` clause, `asFloat`, `=` and `exactValue` are struck (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:381-382`, `.fsi:289-290`); the nine types declare `asFloat` in their apis; 42 declarations give a pair of `=` for each of the 21 coercions, so two numbers of different types compare by value where a coercion joins them, and otherwise by the fallback `a SEQV b` or a written `asFloat(x) = y`; the vector norm is declared at each of the eight types; the reflective generator chooses among the five former listed types. A program's own number type compares by its own `=` or by the fallback. Gated by `tests/NumberOwnTypeNoAsFloatWalk`, `NumberEqualityOwnTypeWalk`, `NumberMixedEqualityWalk`, `VectorNormElementTypesWalk`, `NumberOwnTypeArrayWalk`, `NumberOpenTraitPlusBesideArrayWalk` and the team's `ReflectiveQuickCheckTest`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-number-open/REPORT.md`.
